er; four such layouts are solved per definition, and two `xz_scale` 0 noises, one constant per level seed, choose the layout and one of the square's eight symmetries (`range_choice` at the noise's octiles), so the roller's candidate seeds and the server see the same arrangement and every arrangement holds the same shares. `at` and the named arrangements do not vary with the seed. A site the author fixes is held: each `at` site, and the centre under `core`, lies at least `PIN_MARGIN` (0.1 border units, a tenth of the radius: 68 blocks on a 683-block border) inside its own place, measured exactly from the power bisectors (`PlaceField.reach`), so a spawn or a portal arrival at the site stands inside the place under the default warp of 0.05 border units. A power cell need not contain its site, so where an `at` site is not held the sites move by a capacity-constrained Lloyd iteration (`PlaceField.hold`): each pinned site by the gap from its cell's centroid to the pin, each free site half-way to its centroid, the weights re-solved each round, at most 30 rounds; the compiler refuses the definition, naming the place and the place that reaches over it, when that does not hold. A convex cell clear of a disc of radius m round the centre holds at most (1 − m) / 2 of the square, so no power diagram keeps a small place at the centre beside a neighbour of 45% or more. `core` solves its even ring first and keeps it where the centre holds (every core whose first place is its largest); otherwise the first place is a heart: the disc of radius √(4 share / π) round the centre, its share exactly, and the others wedges from the centre clockwise in list order, the first centred on `direction`, their bearings integrated so each holds its share of the square less the disc. Every wedge meets the heart and reaches the border, so each place is one region. The heart's term is `floor(clamp(|p|² / r², 0, 1)) × (2^24 − 16)`, 0 inside the disc; a wedge's is `floor(clamp(−m × 2^20, 0, 1)) × (2^24 − 1